Stage device-to-host copies in cached host memory, not device memory the host maps.

halide_vulkan_copy_to_host copies the result into a staging buffer, maps it and memcpys it out.
The staging request preferred DEVICE_LOCAL and asked for uncached memory, so on a desktop GPU
with a host-visible device heap (resizable BAR, NVIDIA's 256 MB window) the allocator placed it
in video memory, and the CPU read every byte back uncached across PCIe. Measured on an RTX-class
card with the Linux desktop graph at 1536 x 1024 float RGBA, that read took about five seconds a
frame; with host-cached staging the whole frame develops in about 60 ms. Read-back staging now
prefers HOST_CACHED | HOST_COHERENT memory and no longer prefers DEVICE_LOCAL; any host-visible
type is still accepted when none is cached. Uploads are unchanged. Applies to the pinned
third_party/Halide with the other halide-vulkan-*.patch files.
